[node name="WindowNorth" type="Node3D"]

; Night backdrop sits behind the glass; the figure stands between them.

; The authored window: jamb lining through the 12 cm wall, architrave with a
; bead, a two-rail sash proud of the glass, a projecting sill with a nose, and
; an exterior drip head and ledge. The middle of the aperture is deliberately
; empty — the figure stands behind this glass, and the observation contract
; needs a clear column of sight.
[node name="AuthoredWindow" type="MeshInstance3D" parent="."]
mesh = ExtResource("9_window")

; Night glass: the pane the LoS probe passes through, and what the painted
; backdrop is seen through. Never opaque, never a collider. It sits in the sash
; plane, inside the wall.
;
; It is sized to the WALL OPENING (1.60 x 1.22), not to the sash: at 1.40 wide
; it left 10 cm of open aperture on each side, and at night that read as a
; bright slit of sky beside the frame — the player could see straight past the
; glass to the backdrop. Glass has to fill the hole it is in.
[node name="NightGlass" type="CSGBox3D" parent="."]
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0.038)
cast_shadow = 0
size = Vector3(1.6, 1.22, 0.02)
material = ExtResource("2_glass")

; The sash is glazed two over two (owner's choice, 2026-10-30): a meeting rail
; across the middle and one muntin down it, just proud of the glass on the
; room side. They cast shadows, so the moon lays a cross on the floor, and the
; figure is seen partly through the bars. No collision: the observation rays
; pass the column at x = 0 untouched.
